{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":52340,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"0f8a053a7b3d16d62e64b087e9488c5f259db6ff3dead4e626ae8edc8aa34df5","packTokens":24343,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"platform","tokens":215,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":905,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"contract-new","tokens":474,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"new-look","tokens":82,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"scaffold-rules","tokens":227,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"repomap","tokens":5,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"spec","tokens":15099,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"cb","tokens":397,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"critic","tokens":853,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"approved-design","tokens":5109,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"scaffold","tokens":882,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":5,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"plan"},"pointers":[],"system":"PLATFORM. This product is designed and built as a web app, and nothing else. Where the requirements ask for a mobile app, a phone app, an iOS or Android app, treat them as the same features in one responsive web app that works well on a phone's browser: never stop, park, ask or leave a requirement out because it names a mobile app, and never plan a native app, an app store step or a phone-only frame. Every page is responsive: it is laid out for a phone, a tablet and a desktop width, with touch-sized controls on the narrow one. What only a native app can do (push notifications, background location, a home-screen icon) is given its closest web form (an in-app notice or email, the browser's location prompt, an installable web app) and said so.\n\nYou plan the implementation of an approved spec in a TypeScript web app (React, with the factory's component kit; a new product starts from the scaffold of its approved design).\n- Give at least 2 options (one marked simplest), choose one, and write a decision record of at most 5 lines (adr).\n- Split into tasks TASK-1.. in dependency order. Each task: the requirements it delivers, fileScope (exact repo paths or narrow globs it may change, no overlap between tasks), 1-2 exemplar files to imitate, plannedLoc, approach (short instructions for the implementer).\n- Build in working slices, not layers. A criterion checked through the API or a screen passes only once that route or screen is wired, so the first task that adds a route or screen also wires the app's entry (the startup file, the router, the layout), and each later task adds its logic together with its own endpoint or screen. Code a slice needs (an entity, a helper) goes into the first slice that uses it, not into a task of its own. File scopes may not overlap, so have that first task register routes by convention (file-based routes, or every class of one kind found at startup): later tasks then add only their own files.\n- List a requirement on the task after which its criteria can pass. Do not leave the wiring to a last task that then holds every criterion checked through the app: each earlier task is then checked only there, in the longest and dearest task of the run. Only where the repo's structure forces layers, give the wiring task a dependsOn on every layer it serves.\n- dependsOn: only the tasks whose code this task really needs.\n- complexity, on every task: standard | rules-or-algorithm | external-dependency | compliance-sensitive | real-time | new-to-stack. Most tasks are standard. rules-or-algorithm is a task whose work is mostly business rules, validations, allowed state changes or calculations.\n- Test projects, test files and CI config are not in any file scope: tests are written separately.\n- stubs: for every NEW public type/method/endpoint the tests will call, give a compilable stub file (full file content) whose bodies throw new Error(\"not implemented\"), so tests compile before implementation. Existing APIs need no stubs. Stub paths must be inside a task's fileScope.\n- Outside systems (an email or SMS provider, a payment service, another company's API, a file store): the code reaches each one through one seam, a module of its own (one exported function per call it makes), and nothing else in the app calls the system directly. Use the seam the repo already has for that system; for a new one, give the seam as a stub, so the tests can put a stand-in behind it before the code exists. The system's settings (host, port, sender address, key) are read from configuration by name: put .env.example in the fileScope of the task that writes the seam's real implementation, and say in its approach that each new setting is declared there with an empty value.\n- protectedPathsDeclared: list any migration, CI, build-config or package-feed file you must change (a human will see it).\n- newDependencies: any npm package to add (name, version, registry). Prefer none.\n\nAPI CONTRACT. This product has a web app and an API in two repos, and both are built against one contract.\n- Give one more stub: path \"contracts/openapi.yaml\", content the full OpenAPI 3.0.3 document (YAML) of every operation the approved screens and requirements need. It is outside every task's fileScope: nobody implements it, both sides follow it.\n- Write it in block style, one key per line with two-space indents: no {...} or [...] on a line except an empty value (\"security: []\", \"{}\"). Put any text with an apostrophe, a colon or a # in double quotes. A file with whole operations on one line stops parsing at the first stray quote.\n- No YAML anchors, aliases or merge keys (&name, *name, <<): what is used twice is a schema, a response or a parameter under components, pointed at with $ref.\n- Every operation has an operationId (camelCase), its request body where it takes one, and every status code it answers with.\n- Schemas go under components/schemas with \"required\" lists. Take the field names and types from the approved screens' sample data; an id is an integer.\n- Every JSON response carries an \"example\" with believable data (the approved screens' sample rows): the web app's tests run against these examples.\n- The web app calls the API only through the client generated from this file (lib/api); do not plan a hand-written client, and put no path under lib/api in a stub or in a task's fileScope: the factory generates that folder.\n- What the spec lists as not changing with \"(API-SIDE)\" is behaviour the API side builds and tests: no task here builds it, and the contract still gives its operations and every status code they answer with.\n\nThe approved design is a new look: its implementers get design tokens (colours, type, corners, spacing as CSS variables). Put the app's global stylesheet or theme file in the fileScope of the first task that builds a screen, so the tokens are added once and the other screens use them.\n\nThe approved design is built in next-shadcn: before any task starts, the factory writes the component kit, the theme, the frame and navigation, and every approved page (its blocks, states, layers and text, with the approved sample data) into the repo. Those generated files are not in any task's scope.\n- TASK-1 is the design-system task: its fileScope is exactly the ui-scaffold's designSystemTask.fileScope (plus nothing else UI), and its approach is the designSystemTask.todo list.\n- Then one task per screen in ui-scaffold.screens: its fileScope includes that screen's container (and the API or service files the behaviour needs). The task gives the page real data, API calls, validation and the behaviour the requirements ask for, in the container; it does not restyle or rebuild the page.\n\nRepository map:\n","tools":["read_file","search","repo_map"],"user":"<artifact id=\"spec\" kind=\"spec\" sha=\"\">\n{\n \"assumptions\": [\n  \"Procurement and receiving demo data is imported through a documented mock adapter; a live connector awaits identification of the source system and interface.\",\n  \"Repeated source imports are matched by source identifiers; PO lines and lots can split across lots and pens while retaining allocated animal quantities.\",\n  \"Forecasts use configurable yard target-weight and expected-gain defaults with optional lot-specific overrides.\",\n  \"Configured wastage applies to estimates and projections; actual known wastage is a separate movement, while actual stock consumption is based on delivered quantity.\",\n  \"Ration optimization compares candidate mixes with user-selected nutritional requirements, displays the lowest-cost qualifying mix, and saves only when the user chooses.\",\n  \"A saved plan remains unchanged and is flagged for review after relevant inputs change; an authorized planner confirms or regenerates it.\",\n  \"When stock is insufficient, the feeding save is refused and the web app presents the returned error.\",\n  \"Feed calculations use as-fed quantities; feed totals are converted to pounds for feed-to-gain metrics and dry matter is used for nutrition calculations.\",\n  \"A projected ship date uses the current date plus remaining target weight divided by positive ADG and is unavailable without positive ADG.\",\n  \"Date-range report metrics use feeding and weight records in the selected range; sex is retained when supplied and its filter is unavailable for records without sex data.\",\n  \"Dashboard lot rankings use a user-selected measure; dashboards remain summary-only and lot/report screens provide detail.\",\n  \"Predictive suggestions are explainable rule-based recommendations and do not change saved plans unless a user chooses an action.\",\n  \"Sample records remain distinguishable from operational records, duplicate loading is prevented or surfaced through the returned result, and reset or replacement requires confirmation.\",\n  \"Yard summary ADG and cost of gain use aggregate values across included active lots.\",\n  \"Screen actions and displayed values follow permissions returned for the signed-in user; role descriptions do not establish additional role names or permissions.\",\n  \"Lot profitability is presented as an on-screen filtered list with CSV export rather than a custom report builder.\",\n  \"The API is authoritative for server-side calculations and permissions; the web app displays returned values and refusal messages.\"\n ],\n \"critic\": [\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"REQ-2 covers previewing available mock records and displaying a completed import, but specifies no visible state when the adapter returns no records, fails to load, or rejects the import.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-2\",\n   \"rubric\": 2,\n   \"severity\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"REQ-6 promises inventory-item maintenance but its mutation criterion covers only editing an existing item, leaving no specified way to create the first feed item needed for rations, receipts, and stock-controlled feeding.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-6\",\n   \"rubric\": 6,\n   \"severity\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"AC-6.2 specifies only successful inventory changes, with no error or retained-state behavior if an item edit, receipt, adjustment, or wastage change is rejected or times out.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-6\",\n   \"rubric\": 2,\n   \"severity\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"REQ-8 omits any ration-approval action or permission path even though Q-6 authorizes ration managers to approve definitions and Q-13 removes only the requirement to approve before assignment.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-8\",\n   \"rubric\": 2,\n   \"severity\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"AC-8.1 checks ration-entry fields and AC-8.3 checks an unsaved optimization result, but no criterion covers submitting a new or revised ration and displaying it as a saved ration available for planning.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-8\",\n   \"rubric\": 6,\n   \"severity\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"AC-11.14 rejects only lot allocations whose sum differs from the pen total, so negative per-lot quantities such as −10 and 110 pounds for a 100-pound delivery pass its stated validation.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-11\",\n   \"rubric\": 2,\n   \"severity\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"REQ-13 defines an unavailable projected date for nonpositive ADG but no displayed unavailable state for FCR or cost of gain when a new lot has no gain or feeding data.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-13\",\n   \"rubric\": 2,\n   \"severity\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"REQ-14 permits an authorized planner to act on a sale-timing suggestion and refers to changing a sale date, but no requirement defines an editable sale-date field, its save result, or how it relates to the derived projected ship date.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-14\",\n   \"rubric\": 6,\n   \"severity\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"AC-18.2 says the global sex filter is unavailable 'for records without sex data' without specifying how it behaves when a report contains both known-sex and unknown-sex lots or how those lots appear in filtered exports.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-18\",\n   \"rubric\": 2,\n   \"severity\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"finding\": \"REQ-6 requires displaying returned inventory stock values while AC-20.7 requires operational-only inventory totals from a response containing sample and operational records, without specifying an operational-only returned total or reconciling that requirement with the API-authoritative calculation rule.\",\n   \"reqId\": \"REQ-20\",\n   \"rubric\": 1,\n   \"severity\": \"medium\"\n  }\n ],\n \"lint\": [\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L1 ids\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L2 req-ac\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L3 ears\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L4 vague\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L5 nfr\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L6 scope\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L8 anchors\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L9 size\",\n   \"details\": \"This spec has 22 requirements, about 3 runs' worth of work for a feature; approve it as one run or reject with which part to cut.\",\n   \"passed\": false\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L10 trace\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L11 observable\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  },\n  {\n   \"check\": \"L12 literals\",\n   \"details\": \"ok\",\n   \"passed\": true\n  }\n ],\n \"nfrs\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"NFR-1\",\n   \"metric\": \"At viewport widths of 360 px, 768 px, and 1280 px, 100% of pages render; at 360 px, all interactive controls are operable using touch input.\",\n   \"text\": \"Every page shall render responsively at phone, tablet, and desktop widths, with touch-sized controls on narrow screens.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"outOfScope\": [\n  \"A live procurement-system connector is deferred; the demo uses a documented mock-data import adapter.\",\n  \"Durable persistence and database migration behavior for imported records, mappings, inventory, rations, plans, feeding events, and weight measurements are API-side and not specified by these web requirements.\",\n  \"Authoritative server-side calculation and update rules for inventory movements, ration nutrition and cost, feed-plan quantities, lot rollups, forecast values, and feed-to-gain metrics are not specified by these web requirements.\",\n  \"Authorization enforcement, import identifier matching and deduplication, stock sufficiency decisions, and authoritative yard/profitability data filtering are API-side; these web requirements cover controls, displayed responses, and export contents.\"\n ],\n \"requirements\": [\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The sign-in screen is displayed and the authentication service accepts the submitted credentials and returns the user and permissions\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-1.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned operational landing page and navigation actions allowed by the returned permissions\",\n     \"when\": \"The user submits an email address and password\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The sign-in screen is displayed and the authentication service refuses the submitted credentials\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-1.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned error message and remains on the sign-in page\",\n     \"when\": \"The user submits an email address and password\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"An authenticated screen displays protected data and the session expires or a protected request receives HTTP 401\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-1.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen returns to sign-in and no protected page data remains displayed\",\n     \"when\": \"The web app handles the expired session or 401 response\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide email-and-password sign-in, send the submitted credentials to the sign-in service, display the resulting landing page or authentication error, render navigation and actions according to the signed-in user's returned permissions, and clear authenticated page data and return to sign-in when an authenticated session expires or a protected request returns HTTP 401.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-1\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-1\",\n    \"I-9\",\n    \"Q-2\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"An operationally authorized user opens the import screen and mock adapter records are available\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-2.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays each available record's vendor, source identifier, cost, head count, animal weight, breed, age, source, purchase-order, and receiving fields\",\n     \"when\": \"The user previews the source records\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"An operationally authorized user has selected mock source records\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-2.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The import request contains the selected source records and the screen displays the response status, imported-record list, and returned created or updated counts when supplied\",\n     \"when\": \"The user submits the import\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A user without operational access opens the import screen\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-2.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays imported records and does not show an enabled import action\",\n     \"when\": \"The screen renders using the user's returned permissions\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"Where mock procurement and receiving import is available, the feedlot web app shall let operationally authorized users preview and submit selected records through the documented mock-data adapter and display the returned import result and source fields.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-2\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-1\",\n    \"ASM-1\",\n    \"ASM-23\",\n    \"ASM-24\",\n    \"Q-3\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"An imported PO line is allocated across multiple lots and a lot is allocated across multiple pens\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-3.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen shows each PO-line-to-lot and lot-to-pen relationship with its allocated animal quantity\",\n     \"when\": \"The mapping screen renders the returned mappings\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A mapping response contains pen information\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-3.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays each mapped pen's ID, capacity, current animal count, and mapped allocation quantity\",\n     \"when\": \"The mapping screen renders the response\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"An operationally authorized user changes mapping quantities, or a user without operational access opens the mapping screen\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-3.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the saved mapping values returned by the service, and for a user without operational access it displays existing mappings without mapping-change actions\",\n     \"when\": \"The authorized user saves mappings or the other user's screen renders using returned permissions\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"An edited allocation conflicts with a PO line's returned head count or a pen's returned capacity and the mapping service refuses the save\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-3.4\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned error and the last saved mapping values remain displayed\",\n     \"when\": \"The user submits the mapping changes\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall let operationally authorized users map PO lines to one or more lots and lots to one or more pens, retain allocated animal quantities, and display the resulting traceability and pen information.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-3\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-1\",\n    \"ASM-2\",\n    \"ASM-25\",\n    \"Q-3\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The import response identifies source records as matched to existing records\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-4.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the matched record list and response status without displaying those records as newly created duplicates\",\n     \"when\": \"The repeated import completes\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A repeated-import response updates a PO line's head count below its existing mapped allocation quantities and returns an allocation conflict\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-4.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the matched record, returned head count, existing mapped quantities, and an allocation-conflict flag without presenting the record as a new duplicate\",\n     \"when\": \"The repeated import completes\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"When an operational user repeats an import containing previously imported source-system identifiers, the feedlot web app shall display the import result for the matched records returned by the import service without presenting them as newly created duplicates, and flag returned allocation conflicts when an updated head count is below existing mapped quantities.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-4\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-1\",\n    \"ASM-24\",\n    \"Q-3\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 0.3333333333333333\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The forecast response contains incoming animal data, yard defaults, and a lot-specific override\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-5.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned incoming data, target weight, expected gain rate, projected market date, and forecast basis using the lot-specific override\",\n     \"when\": \"A user opens the lot forecast\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A lot forecast has no positive gain rate available\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-5.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The projected date field displays an unavailable value on the screen\",\n     \"when\": \"The user opens the forecast\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"An authorized manager changes a yard default or lot-specific override, or a user without manager authorization opens forecast settings\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-5.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned forecast values for saved changes, and does not show assumption-edit actions to a user without manager authorization\",\n     \"when\": \"The manager saves the assumption or the other user's screen renders\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display market-date and target-weight forecasts from incoming animal data and configured yard defaults, use lot-specific overrides when present, show a forecast as unavailable without a positive gain rate, and expose forecast-assumption editing only to authorized managers.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-5\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-2\",\n    \"ASM-3\",\n    \"ASM-33\",\n    \"Q-4\"\n   ],\n   \"stability\": 1\n  },\n  {\n   \"acceptance\": [\n    {\n     \"given\": \"The inventory response contains feed items and stock movements\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-6.1\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays each item's name, category, available quantity, unit, unit cost, dry-matter percentage, energy value, storage location, wastage setting when returned, and opening, received, consumed, and closing stock values\",\n     \"when\": \"A user opens the inventory screen\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"An authorized inventory user records a receipt or adjustment, edits an item, or changes wastage settings\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-6.2\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays the saved inventory record and stock-movement values returned by the service\",\n     \"when\": \"The user submits the change\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"given\": \"A user has inventory viewing access but lacks inventory-edit access\",\n     \"id\": \"AC-6.3\",\n     \"level\": \"ui\",\n     \"then\": \"The screen displays inventory values and does not show enabled item, receipt, adjustment, or wastage-edit actions\",\n     \"when\": \"The user opens inventory\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display feed inventory items and stock movements and let authorized inventory users maintain items, record receipts or adjustments, and change wastage settings, while displaying inventory details to users with viewing access.\",\n   \"id\": \"REQ-6\",\n   \"op\": \"ADDED\",\n   \"sources\": [\n    \"I-3\",\n    \"ASM-4\",\n    \"ASM-28\",\n    \"ASM-34\",\n    \